# DNS Daddy — Docker Compose environment.
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#   $EDITOR .env
#   docker compose up -d
#
# .env is gitignored: it holds addresses specific to your deployment.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# REQUIRED on a public VPS.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Which source addresses may resolve. Anything else is REFUSED before any
# upstream work happens.
#
# The application's built-in default is loopback plus the private ranges. That
# is right for a resolver on a LAN and WRONG for a VPS: your clients arrive
# from public addresses, so leaving this at the default makes every real query
# fail with REFUSED while the dashboard still looks perfectly healthy.
#
# Use the same source addresses your firewall allows on port 53, and keep:
#   127.0.0.0/8      loopback, for health checks
#   172.16.0.0/12    the Docker bridge gateway, or the published port breaks
#
# Example for two sites:
# DNSDADDY_ALLOWED_CLIENT_CIDRS=127.0.0.0/8,172.16.0.0/12,203.0.113.42/32,198.51.100.10/32
DNSDADDY_ALLOWED_CLIENT_CIDRS=127.0.0.0/8,172.16.0.0/12

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Reverse proxy (leave blank if the dashboard is loopback-only with no proxy)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Peers whose X-Forwarded-For / X-Real-IP / X-Forwarded-Proto are believed.
#
# Counter-intuitive but important: with Caddy on the host proxying to
# 127.0.0.1:8080, the container does NOT see 127.0.0.1. Docker's port
# publishing rewrites the source to the bridge gateway, so this must be the
# bridge network — not loopback.
#
# Find yours:  docker network inspect bridge -f '{{(index .IPAM.Config 0).Subnet}}'
# DNSDADDY_TRUSTED_PROXY_CIDRS=172.17.0.0/16

# Session cookie Secure flag: auto | always | never.
# Set "always" once TLS is in front, so it can never cross plain HTTP.
# DNSDADDY_SECURE_COOKIES=always

# Public URL, used to build the DoH URLs shown on the Setup page.
# DNSDADDY_BASE_URL=https://dns.example.co.uk

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Behavioural detection
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# On by default. Nothing here blocks anything — the detectors observe, score,
# explain and alert; enforcement stays with policy and threat feeds. Every
# detector is experimental. See docs/detection/README.md.
#
# DNSDADDY_DETECTION_ENABLED=true

# info | low | medium | high. Findings below this are not stored.
# DNSDADDY_DETECTION_MIN_SEVERITY=low

# Write findings as newline-delimited JSON for a log shipper to tail. A
# relative path lands inside the data volume. Off by default: this is browsing
# history in a second place. See docs/siem.md.
# DNSDADDY_DETECTION_FINDINGS_FILE=findings.jsonl

# Parent domains exempt from behavioural findings, on top of the built-in list.
# Reputation services, CDNs and mail gateways legitimately produce traffic
# shaped exactly like a DNS tunnel. This never affects whether a domain
# resolves.
# DNSDADDY_DETECTION_EXCLUDED_DOMAINS=reputation.your-vendor.example,gateway.your-filter.example

# How long findings are kept. They outlive the query log on purpose.
# DNSDADDY_DETECTION_RETENTION_DAYS=30

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Optional
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# TZ=UTC
# DNSDADDY_VERSION=dev
